# Requirements — Planning
#
# Process Groups: A Practice Guide, 5.2 Collect Requirements and 5.1 Plan Scope Management.
# The table is a requirements traceability matrix in miniature.

screen: requirements
title: Requirements
phase: Planning
process: 5.2 Collect Requirements
order: 30
requires: charter
teaches: |
  How to turn a sponsor's intent into conditions someone can build and test
  against, and why every requirement needs acceptance criteria written at the
  same time. By the end you should be able to explain the difference between a
  requirement and a wish.
before: |
  The charter has to be signed. Detailed requirements are Planning work, and
  Planning happens on an authorized project. Gathering them earlier means
  gathering them for something that may not go ahead.
after: |
  The scope statement and the WBS. A requirement with no work package behind
  it will not get built; a work package with no requirement behind it is
  someone's idea rather than the project's.
intro: |
  Requirements are the conditions the deliverable has to meet. This is where
  the charter's high-level requirements get broken down into things you can
  actually build and test.

  Each requirement carries its own acceptance criteria, because a requirement
  nobody can test is an opinion.
source: |
  Process Groups: A Practice Guide (PMI, 2022), process 5.2 Collect Requirements, in the Planning
  process group. Outputs: the requirements documentation and the requirements
  traceability matrix, which is what the table below is.

field items
  prompt: What conditions does the deliverable have to meet? One per row, each with a way to test it.
  check rows>=3: Three requirements is the minimum for this to be worth a table. Break the charter's high-level requirements down.
  check column:acceptance: A requirement with no acceptance criteria is an opinion. Say how you would prove it was met.
  label: Requirements
  type: table
  origin: standard
  view: log
  title_column: requirement
  tone_column: status
  source: |
    Requirements documentation (5.2 output), plus the requirements traceability
    matrix that links each requirement back to the objective it serves.
  why: |
    Each row is one testable condition. The priority column is what you argue
    about when the schedule gets tight: MoSCoW (Must, Should, Could, Won't) is
    the common scheme because it forces a real ranking instead of everything
    being "high".

    The "traces to" column is the traceability matrix: it links the requirement
    back to a charter objective, so you can prove every requirement earns its
    place and every objective is covered.
  if_blank: |
    The build is steered by whoever remembers the conversation best, and
    finished work gets rejected for missing a condition nobody wrote down.
  example: |
    R1 | Every field shows its source | Functional | Must | Focus any field and the panel names its PMBOK process | Charter §3
  column ref
    label: ID
    type: line
    width: 1
  column requirement
    label: Requirement
    type: text
    width: 5
  column kind
    label: Type
    type: choice
    options: Functional, Non-functional, Business, Stakeholder, Transition, Quality
    width: 2
  column priority
    label: Priority
    type: choice
    options: Must=danger, Should=accent, Could=neutral, Won't=faint
    width: 1
  column acceptance
    label: Acceptance criteria
    type: text
    width: 5
  column traces
    label: Traces to
    type: line
    width: 2
  column status
    label: Status
    type: choice
    options: Proposed=faint, Agreed=accent, Built=ok, Verified=ok, Dropped=danger
    width: 2

field assumptions
  prompt: What are you treating as true without proof?
  check filled: Plans rest on beliefs nobody stated. Writing one down turns a silent bet into something that can be checked, or into a risk.
  label: Assumptions
  type: text
  origin: standard
  source: |
    Assumption log (4.1 output, maintained throughout). Assumptions and
    constraints are recorded together because both limit what the plan can be.
  why: |
    An assumption is something you are treating as true without proof. Writing
    it down converts a silent bet into something that can be checked, and into
    a risk if it turns out to be wrong.
  if_blank: |
    Plans rest on beliefs nobody stated, and when one turns out to be false the
    failure looks like bad luck instead of an unchecked assumption.
  example: |
    The delivery machine keeps a working Qt 6.8 toolchain for the length of the
    project.

field constraints
  prompt: What fixed limits does this project have to work inside?
  check filled: Name the dates, budgets, platforms and rules you do not get to negotiate, or the plan will quietly assume them away.
  label: Constraints
  type: text
  origin: standard
  source: |
    Charter and 5.2 Collect Requirements: the limiting factors the project has
    to work inside.
  why: |
    A constraint is a fixed limit you do not get to negotiate: a date, a
    budget, a platform, a regulation. Naming them stops the plan from quietly
    assuming them away.
  if_blank: |
    The plan promises something the constraints make impossible, and the
    conflict surfaces as a missed date.
  example: |
    Windows only. $0 budget. No code signing certificate available.
